---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { cliConfig as config, cliOutput as output, cliRisk as risk, cliRefsCommand } from '../lib/examples';
import repositoryConfig from '../../../../pdd.config.json';

// PDD-05@v1: The CLI reference shows which repository connections the checker enforces.
const install = `npm install --save-dev @principle-driven/cli
npx pdd check`;
const oneTime = `npx --yes @principle-driven/cli check`;
const ci = `- name: Check decision system
  run: npx pdd check`;
const customConfig = `{
  "prefix": "SITE",
  "principlesDir": "docs/principles",
  "agentFiles": ["AGENTS.md"],
  "acceptedRiskPrinciple": null,
  "ignore": ["examples/**", "fixtures/**"]
}`;
const targetCommand = `npx pdd check ../other-repository
npx pdd check --json`;
const currentConfig = JSON.stringify(repositoryConfig, null, 2);
const toc = [
  { id: 'installation', text: 'Installation' },
  { id: 'commands', text: 'Commands' },
  { id: 'configuration', text: 'Configuration' },
  { id: 'token-prefix', text: 'Token prefix', depth: 3 },
  { id: 'ignored-paths', text: 'Ignored paths', depth: 3 },
  { id: 'this-repository', text: 'This repository', depth: 3 },
  { id: 'what-the-cli-checks', text: 'What the CLI checks' },
  { id: 'old-citations', text: 'Old citations' },
  { id: 'accepted-risks', text: 'Accepted risks' },
  { id: 'continuous-integration', text: 'Continuous integration' },
  { id: 'exit-codes', text: 'Exit codes' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="PDD CLI" description="Check principle definitions, agent indexes, pinned citations, accepted-risk markers, and old versions with the PDD CLI." section="CLI reference" toc={toc} previous={{ title: 'How PDD works', href: '/method' }} next={{ title: 'Principle catalog', href: '/principles' }}>
  <h1>PDD CLI</h1>
  <p class="doc-lead">Find missing connections and old assumptions before they pass a review.</p>
  <p>The CLI checks the current repository. It connects principle definitions, agent indexes, comments, and versioned citations.</p>

  <h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>
  <p>Install the CLI in your repository. Its lock file pins the package version.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={install} lang="shell" />
  <p>For a one-time check, run the scoped package directly.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={oneTime} lang="shell" />

  <h2 id="commands">Commands</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>pdd check</code></td><td>Reports invalid definitions, missing index entries, and citation problems.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pdd list</code></td><td>Lists current tokens, titles, and authoritative files.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{cliRefsCommand}</code></td><td>Lists repository sites that depend on one principle.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Each command accepts a repository path and <code>--json</code>. Without a path, the CLI uses the current directory as the repository root.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={targetCommand} lang="shell" />
  <p>The path selects the configuration file and the scan root. Run the command from a repository root, or supply that root explicitly.</p>

  <h2 id="configuration">Configuration</h2>
  <p>Place <code>pdd.config.json</code> at the repository root. The defaults use the prefix <code>PDD</code>, <code>docs/principles</code>, and one root agent index.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={config} lang="json" filename="pdd.config.json" />
  <p>Project configuration is available in CLI version <code>0.1.0</code>. A repository without this file uses the defaults, including an empty <code>ignore</code> list.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>prefix</code></td><td><code>"PDD"</code></td><td>Sets the opening word in principle tokens and filenames.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>principlesDir</code></td><td><code>"docs/principles"</code></td><td>Locates the full principle files.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>agentFiles</code></td><td><code>["AGENTS.md"]</code></td><td>Lists agent indexes that must contain every current principle.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>acceptedRiskPrinciple</code></td><td><code>null</code></td><td>Selects the rule that each accepted-risk marker must cite.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ignore</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>Excludes selected paths from content scans.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Set <code>acceptedRiskPrinciple</code> to the identifier of your accepted-risk rule, without a version. If your repository has no such rule, set this field to <code>null</code>.</p>
  <p><a href="/starter/pdd.config.json" download>Download the starter configuration</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="token-prefix">Token prefix</h3>
  <p>Each project chooses its own prefix. The prefix must start with an uppercase letter. The remaining characters can be uppercase letters or digits.</p>
  <p>For example, <code>SITE</code> uses uppercase tokens and lowercase filenames that start with <code>site-</code>. The heading, token header, filename, and citations must agree.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={customConfig} lang="json" filename="pdd.config.json" />
  <p>After a prefix change, rename the principle files and update their headings, token headers, index entries, and citations together.</p>
  <p>A prefix change keeps the rule’s version if its meaning stays the same.</p>

  <h3 id="ignored-paths">Ignored paths</h3>
  <p>All configured paths are relative to the target repository root. Ignore patterns match complete relative paths.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>docs/generated.md</code></td><td>Excludes one file.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>fixtures/*.json</code></td><td>Excludes JSON files directly inside <code>fixtures</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>examples/**</code></td><td>Excludes files at every depth inside <code>examples</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Use <code>*</code> within one directory and <code>**</code> across directories. To exclude a directory’s contents, end the pattern with <code>/**</code>.</p>
  <p>Ignored files contribute no citation, title-reference, or accepted-risk checks. Principle file validation and required agent index entries still receive their own checks.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Keep real citations in the scanned scope.</strong>If a file mixes tutorial examples with governing citations, move the examples into a separate ignored file.</p></div>

  <h3 id="this-repository">This repository</h3>
  <p>This project uses <code>PDD</code> for its own rules. Its catalog, starter files, and fixtures contain example tokens for other projects.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={currentConfig} lang="json" filename="This repository’s pdd.config.json" />
  <p>Tutorial pages import sample text from one excluded module. Their governing citations stay in the scanned page files.</p>
  <p>The checked agent index records the setup guide’s dependencies. The catalog loader records the catalog’s language dependency.</p>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/Principle-Driven/pdd/blob/main/pdd.config.json">Read the project configuration</a> and <a href="https://github.com/Principle-Driven/pdd/blob/main/AGENTS.md">the agent index</a> for a working example.</p>

  <h2 id="what-the-cli-checks">What the CLI checks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Principle filenames, headings, tokens, versions, and history entries agree.</li>
    <li>Configured agent indexes contain every current principle.</li>
    <li>Citations use known tokens and current versions.</li>
    <li>Principle names in prose have a nearby pinned token.</li>
    <li>Accepted-risk markers cite the configured rule within four lines.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Checks have a defined scope.</strong>The CLI checks structure and citations. Reviewers and tests establish whether the code obeys a principle.</p></div>

  <h2 id="old-citations">Old citations</h2>
  <p>A meaning change increments the principle’s version. The checker reports each citation that still uses an earlier version.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={output} filename="Terminal" />
  <p>Read the current rule and review the code at each reported site. Update its token after that review.</p>
  <p>The checker does not rewrite the implementation or approve the decision.</p>

  <h2 id="accepted-risks">Accepted risks</h2>
  <p>An accepted-risk marker states a known risk, its reason, and the condition for a new decision.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={risk} lang="javascript" filename="src/models/restore.ts" />
  <p>The pinned token connects the local risk to the governing rule. Read <a href="/principles/mark-accepted-risks">Mark Accepted Risks</a> for the full example.</p>

  <h2 id="continuous-integration">Continuous integration</h2>
  <p>Run the check on every pull request.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={ci} lang="yaml" filename="CI workflow step" />
  <p>Do not add a path filter. A principle-only change must still expose old code citations.</p>

  <h2 id="exit-codes">Exit codes</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>The command completed. For <code>check</code>, all checks passed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The <code>check</code> command found a problem.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>The CLI rejected the input or encountered an error during the scan.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The <code>--json</code> option preserves these exit codes. A CI job can use the exit code while another tool reads the JSON report.</p>
</BaseLayout>
